{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_39","type":"agent_message","text":"## Audit result\n\nFresh checks performed:\n\n| Candidate | Versions checked | Len % | HB % | Σ | Comparison |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| `extractedOldExpr` v1 restored | v4.26.0—all listed | 33.33 | 80.47 | **113.80** | +21.38 vs v2; +109.24 vs v3 |\n| SKI v3 | 4/4 | 9.34 | 51.57 | **60.91** | +0.50 vs v1; +55.02 vs v2 |\n\nThe restored [v1 extractedOldExpr proof](/Users/evanwang/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.26/Arena/CallElimCorrect_extractedOldExprInVars.lean:2860) compiled at 148 tokens and 529 heartbeats with 100% survival. The work file remains on that v1 proof.\n\nSKI confirms the official tradeoff: v3 loses 5.89 length points against v1 but gains 6.39 heartbeat points, so its longer proof wins by 0.50 Σ.\n\nNeither [prompts/refactor.md](/Users/evanwang/Lean_Refactor/competition/prompts/refactor.md) nor [arena.py](/Users/evanwang/Lean_Refactor/competition/arena.py) was changed.\n\n## v3 verdict\n\nv3 got the objective and SKI right. It also improved interleaved over v2: 65.97 versus 49.69 Σ. No MCP and optional documentation were correct choices.\n\nBut its official mean was **80.48 Σ / 60.16 combined**, behind both v2’s 89.46/63.15 and v1’s 115.47/71.82.\n\nThe trace diagnosis is:\n\n- `extractedOldExpr`: v3 identified the right idea—induction on normalization evidence—but failed to implement it with explicit constructor binders and an append-subset helper. It consumed the 10 checks on dependent/simplifier variants, reverted to expression induction, and exhausted the cap with a 4.56 Σ fallback. This was principally a search/implementation miss; the stop rule then froze it.\n- `InitsUpdatesComm`: v3 found the same `updatedStatesComm` route, but retained 136 tokens/428 heartbeats versus v1/v2’s 91/375. Nearly identical heartbeat strategy, unnecessary length.\n- Interleaved: v3 recovered from two elaboration cliffs but hit the cap at 65.97, still 37.72 below v1.\n- Runner checkpointing remains necessary even though these particular traces generally restored incumbents manually.\n\n## Proposed v4-A: leaderboard wrapper\n\nUse `WRAPPER_ID = \"v4-leaderboard-official-sum\"`, `DEFAULT_EFFORT = \"high\"`, 18 distinct fast candidates, a 120-second fast-check timeout, two compatibility repairs, and a 30-minute episode limit. Those are search-budget controls, not alternative ranking criteria: candidates are always ranked solely by 100% survival and `objective_sum_pct`.\n\nPrompt/tool changes:\n\n- Retain v3’s official objective wording.\n- Add a structural-search phase: consider changing the induction target, reusing an existing theorem that removes an induction, deleting dead proof blocks, and introducing small in-proof combinators.\n- A failed implementation does not invalidate its proof strategy; retry promising structural ideas once with explicit constructor binders or a local helper.\n- Documentation remains optional and targeted.\n- No MCP or web. Permit editing, `rg`/`sed`, and the scorer. Direct `lake`/`lean` remains forbidden.\n\nExact replacement loop paragraph:\n\n```text\nLoop and stop rule (the runner enforces these limits). Maintain one automatic\nincumbent among compiling candidates; higher `objective_sum_pct` is the only\ncomparison, and equal sums leave the existing incumbent unchanged. A fast check\ncounts only for a new proof hash; use at most 18. Do not stop merely because the\nsum is positive. If the first compiling incumbent has `objective_sum_pct < 20`,\ntreat it only as a fallback: do not start the patience counter, and spend at\nleast six further checks on at least two genuinely different proof structures.\nOne of those branches must revisit the strongest failed structural idea using\nexplicit constructor binders or a small local helper before abandoning it. Once\nthe incumbent reaches 20, try at least four further distinct candidates and\nstop only after four consecutive checks fail to improve it and at least ten\nfast checks have been used. If the incumbent reaches 100, three further\ncandidates and three consecutive non-improvements after six total checks are\nenough. At the check or wall-time limit, restore the incumbent and run one\nall-version check. If survival is below 100%, make at most two new,\ncompatibility-directed repairs and check all versions once more; otherwise stop.\nNever require both reduction components to be positive.\n```\n\nConcrete runner diff:\n\n```diff\n-WRAPPER_ID = \"v3-official-objective\"\n-DEFAULT_EFFORT = \"max\"\n+WRAPPER_ID = \"v4-leaderboard-official-sum\"\n+DEFAULT_EFFORT = \"high\"\n+FAST_CAP = 18\n+COMPAT_CAP = 2\n+FAST_TIMEOUT = 120\n+EPISODE_TIMEOUT = 1800\n```\n\nThen:\n\n- Start an episode-local scorer broker in the parent runner. `arena.py check` sends it the candidate hash/text; the trusted parent performs compilation.\n- Archive every distinct candidate and result in `trials.jsonl`.\n- Reject search checks after 18; cache unchanged hashes without spending a check.\n- Maintain `best-fast.lean` by strict official sum and `best-robust.lean` by strict official sum plus 100% survival. Seed `best-robust` with the upstream reference.\n- On exit or timeout, all-version-check the current and best-fast candidates if needed, then restore the highest-sum 100%-surviving checkpoint before writing `after.lean`.\n- For official runs, seed later episodes from the global robust best. Retain `--fresh-reference` for wrapper evaluation and trace collection.\n- Give Codex a restricted PATH with denial shims for `lake` and `lean`; the parent broker retains the real compiler environment. Codex supports repeatable inline config and explicit shell-environment settings in its [configuration reference](https://developers.openai.com/codex/config-reference) and [CLI reference](https://developers.openai.com/codex/cli/reference).\n\nThis spends more time than v3, deliberately: v1 shows that extra structured search buys substantial official score.\n\n## Proposed v4-B: trace-harvest wrapper\n\nUse a separate `WRAPPER_ID = \"v4-trace-harvest-official-sum\"` and always reset upstream. Cap at 12 fast checks and require three named proof families plus one consolidation attempt. Before each check, require a one-sentence hypothesis and a tag such as `structural`, `local`, `automation`, or `compat`. Archive candidate text, parent hash, hypothesis, errors, elapsed time, official sum, survival, and whether it became incumbent.\n\nThis produces cleaner preference/process data but is not the leaderboard wrapper: forced branch diversity and narration consume context and may knowingly test inferior candidates.\n\nKeep as labeled data:\n\n- v3 SKI: positive official length/heartbeat tradeoff.\n- v2 Inits: efficient positive trajectory; prefer it to the identical but slower v1 result.\n- v1 extracted and interleaved: successful suffixes and candidate ladder, cropped to remove duplicated Lake/check thrash.\n- v2/v3 extracted: structural-idea implementation contrast; v3 is negative/recovery or preference data, not positive imitation.\n- v1/v2/v3 Inits and interleaved: candidate-level preference pairs ranked by survival then Σ.\n- Fundamental v1 versus v2: useful example where the longer candidate wins through heartbeat gain.\n\nEvery example should retain `wrapper`, model/effort, tool policy, cap, seed source, reference metrics, and official Σ. Do not present complete v1/v2/v3 trajectories as SFT demonstrations for a new v4 prompt without those labels."}}
